CREATED:20260904T140116Z
DESCRIPTION:In honor of Trinity Humanities & Arts "Belonging?" and Duke's 
 We the People: Project 250\, the Department of Religious Studies will be 
 addressing the possibilities and predicaments of religious belonging\, wi
 th particular attention to "religious freedom" as a peculiarly American v
 alue and preoccupation. \nJoin us for the first event in this series with
  guest speaker David Krueger\, with opening remarks from Imhotep Newsome.
  Moderated by Neena Mahadev.\n\n"Promise\, Paradox\, and Psalm 137: The P
 olitics of Frederick Douglass's Speech\, 'What to the Slave Is the Fourth
  of July?'" - Imhotep Newsome\n\nAbout the speaker:\nImhotep A. Newsome i
 s a Ph.D. candidate in Religion at Duke University\, specializing in Hebr
 ew Bible/Old Testament with a minor in Black Church Studies. He holds an 
 M.Div. from Howard University School of Divinity and a B.A. in Classics f
 rom Howard University. At Duke\, he taught in the Thompson Writing Progra
 m and is also a Bass Instructional Fellow. He serves as Co-Coordinator fo
 r Teaching & Learning in Duke's Graduate Program in Religion and previous
 ly served as Graduate Student Chaplain for Duke Divinity School's Black S
 eminarians Union.\n"The Promise of Pluralism: Religion and Freedom at Ame
 rica 250" - David Krueger\n\nAbout the speaker:\nDavid M. Krueger is the 
 executive director of the Dialogue Institute at Temple University and co-
 editor of the Journal of Ecumenical Studies. He is a scholar and educator
  who is passionate about public history\, religious literacy\, and civic 
 dialogue. \n\nHis areas of academic expertise include American religious 
 history\, pluralism\, race\, and ethnicity. Dr. Krueger is a sought-after
  lecturer and speaker and has frequently served as a narrator and scholar
 ly contributor on the Science Channel. He received an M.Div from Palmer T
 heological Seminary\, a ThM from Princeton Theological Seminary\, and a P
 hD in religious studies from Temple University. \n\nHis book\, Myths of t
 he Rune Stone: Viking Martyrs and the Birthplace of America\, was publish
 ed by the University of Minnesota Press. Dr. Krueger is a versatile and s
 easoned educator who has taught at several area universities and communit
 y-based settings. \n\nThis event is supported by Belonging? a contributio
 n by the Arts and Humanities departments of Trinity College to We the Peo
 ple: Duke and Durham Initiative. Belonging? is funded by Trinity College 
 and Duke Arts.
DURATION:PT0S
DTSTAMP:20260910T140133Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260914T160000
LAST-MODIFIED:20260910T140133Z
