CREATED:20260827T165841Z
DESCRIPTION:Around 2 billion people worldwide live in areas affected by ar
 med conflict\, including dozens of ongoing wars\, sectarian violence and 
 political instability that forces the displacement of millions of people.
  Beyond the direct impacts of these conflicts on human health\, including
  the many people who are killed or injured as a result of armed combat\, 
 prolonged periods of conflict can have much wider health impacts\, such a
 s by worsening environmental conditions that drive disease and disrupting
  access to medical services and care.\n\nThis Think Global panel will exp
 lore these wider impacts of armed conflict on health\, including how disr
 uptions to health systems pose challenges for treatment of chronic diseas
 es and other health conditions. Panelists will offer firsthand perspectiv
 es of the obstacles they have faced in delivering healthcare in areas aff
 ected by conflict and explore the impact these challenges have on both pa
 tients and providers. The discussion will seek to define how global healt
 h research can measure and articulate the impacts of conflict on human he
 alth\, as well as ways that those impacts may be avoided.
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260915T130000
DTSTAMP:20260827T170636Z
